\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{How the notes are organized}

\p{The main development proceeds from a general construction to increasingly
specific puzzles, then to solving and play. The following guide describes the
whole intended route. The chapters currently collected here develop the
general puzzle action; the later stages remain directions for development.
The linked overviews explain the questions at each stage.}

\p{\strong{1. General Rubik puzzles.} Begin with
[incidence, symmetry and moves](frap-0002), before choosing a layered cube.
The first developed chapter, [polytopes, flags and facet symmetry](frap-0008),
introduces the abstract objects and the symmetry needed to extend a facet
rotation. [Turning a facet into a puzzle move](frap-0009) then defines the
local action and proves that it is a permutation. Tetrahedron and cube
examples make the general definitions calculable.
[Positions, orientations and global constraints](frap-000A) separates
the destination of a piece from transport in its local frame.
[Transfer and the effect of dimension](frap-000B) connects that transport
with a group homomorphism and computes the relevant local abelianizations.}

\p{\strong{2. The family of layered cubes.} The
[#{N\times N\times N} family](frap-0003) requires additional piece data and
layer turns. This stage will explain that model and its relation to the
general construction, including what changes with #{N}. Abstract dimension
and the number of physical layers are different parameters.}

\p{\strong{3. The three-layer cube.} The
[#{3\times3\times3} specialization](frap-0004) will make positions,
orientations and reachability explicit. It supplies the detailed state
description on which the treatment of solving methods will depend.}

\p{\strong{4. Human methods and computer algorithms.} The study of
[why solution methods work](frap-0005) will connect recognizable cases and
move sequences with their algebraic effects. It will distinguish the
organization and usability of a human solve from search, move costs and
computational optimality.}

\p{\strong{5. Return to play.} [Using and exploring move sequences](frap-0006)
will bring notation, scrambling, recognition, replay and complete solves
together. The aim is to make the earlier mathematics useful in understanding
and using the puzzle.}

\p{[Further mathematical connections](frap-0007) accompany this route.
Orientation and transfer grow out of the general move action; transport and
topology offer a related geometric investigation; probability and
representation theory enter through scrambling and search. These branches
will state their own prerequisites and need not all be read before continuing
to concrete cubes. The developed chapters begin below, with the general
incidence structure.}
